VLMEvalKit is UNIMPORTABLE ON WINDOWS at the revision this campaign pinned.

    vlmeval/dataset/utils/hipho_verifier.py

        def timeout(timeout_seconds: int = 10):
            if os.name == "posix":
                ...
                return decorator
            # <-- no else branch: returns None on nt

    ...and line 681 is `@timeout(timeout_seconds=30)`, so importing anything
    from `vlmeval` raises:

        TypeError: 'NoneType' object is not callable

This is an upstream defect, not a configuration problem: `import vlmeval` fails
on any non-posix platform. The fix is the missing else branch — a no-op
decorator, which is what the posix path degrades to anyway whenever it is
called off the main thread (the function already handles that case by executing
without a signal).

WHY THIS IS RECORDED RATHER THAN JUST APPLIED
---------------------------------------------
A patched reference is not the published reference, and Arm 1's whole job is to
reproduce a published row. Any ledger line produced through a patched
VLMEvalKit must be readable as such years later, so the patch lives in the repo
next to the adapters and is named in the plan (§7).

BLAST RADIUS, stated rather than assumed: the decorator guards
`answer_tag_reward_fn_for_r1`, the math-answer verifier used by the HiPhO
dataset. None of OCRBench / DocVQA / ChartQA / TextVQA / MMStar call it. On
Windows the patched decorator makes the wrapped function run WITHOUT a 30s
timeout — identical to what the posix path already does on a non-main thread —
so a pathological input hangs instead of raising. That is a real behaviour
change and it is why this note exists; it does not touch any code path the
Argus corpora exercise.

